  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Overall synoptic pattern has changed little from\nyesterday with frontal boundary still stalled from roughly\nWilmington into Onslow Bay. However, precipitation coverage this\nmorning is far more limited with stronger forcing displace to\nour west despite ample moisture. All guidance shows an uptick in\nshower coverage with embedded thunderstorm coverage after about\n08-09z as shortwave trough currently over eastern TN provides\nthe necessarily support, with highest coverage likely filling in\nover the Outer Banks and towards the Crystal Coast.\n\nUptick of showers and thunderstorms will continue into the\ndaytime hours, becoming enhanced late morning into the \nafternoon with slight building of instability. Ensemble of hi-\nres guidance gives the highest probabilities of heaviest\nrainfall lingering along the southern coast, especially in the\nafternoon with enhanced low-level convergence along a sea breeze\nboundary. However, there is still some spread in where axis of\nhighest precipitation totals reside with a few members showing\nhigher amounts displaced farther north. Given this continued\nuncertainty, the existing Flood Watch was maintained through\ntonight for all areas. The odds of seeing the highest \nprecipitation amounts are a subjective 60-75% across the \nsouthern half of the FA and 25-40% across the northern half.\n\nAdditional rainfall amounts of 1-2 inches are expected today,\nmost likely from the Outer Banks southwestward along the Crystal\nCoast. In locations where training occurs, locally higher\namounts of 3-5 inches remain possible in a reasonable worst case\nscenario. Within the watch area, there will continue to be a \nrisk of minor/nuisance flooding, but with a continued risk of a \nfew instances of flash flooding. Additionally, we've received \nsome reports of trees coming down in the absence of much wind, \nand this is likely due to saturated soils, not strong winds. \nThis may continue to be a risk, especially for areas that have \nseen the most rainfall over the past 24-36 hours.\n\nOverall severe risk appears low today, but cannot rule out a\nweakly rotating storm or two closer to the frontal boundary\nwhich could produce a brief spin up and gusty winds up to 30-40\nmph at times.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...With Sunday and Monday trending drier with the \nlingering front finally getting carried offshore, focus then \nturns to another front approaching the eastern CONUS next mid- \nweek. This boundary appears weaker and more progressive than our\ncurrent boundary but will still provide a focus for showers and\nthunderstorms. Ahead of the boundary, strengthening southwesterly\nflow and building low-level thicknesses will bring a return of \noppressive heat with at least a couple days of heat indices \napproaching or exceeding 105F possible. \n\nThere is also an improvement in mid-level kinematics with this \nboundary and there is elevated potential for some stronger \nstorms, primarily posing a damaging wind gust risk. This will be\nbe dependent on frontal timing which remains uncertain, but\ntrends will need to be monitored.",
